    "description": "Long before the proliferation of tablets and smartphones, the Fisher Price Cassette Player & Recorder from 1987 allowed children to play around with recording various sounds onto a cassette, as well as playing them back. This briefcase-looking toy, similar to a boombox, comes with oversized and color-coded buttons for ease of use, and red accents that mark important components such as the cassette compartment, microphone, and volume level. This is a toy of endless fun and imagination. I can imagine children being fascinated with their own voice, playing with various cadences and intonations that they produce (that’s what I would have been doing). Or, perhaps, trying to record all kinds of sounds they could possibly imagine - the running water, walking on grass, loud clangs and bangs, and silent steps. What makes this toy more than just a plaything is that it can be used as a regular cassette playing device. Storytelling cassettes for children and children’s music were immensely popular in the 80s and 90s, expanding the use cases of this toy even further. Additionally, this toy is pedagogical since it teaches children about how sound and audio recording work. A keen ear would notice how being close or far from the microphone changes the volume and quality of recordings. This media artifact serves as an example of a child-friendly interface that mimicked real-world technologies.\n\n\n- Omair",
